  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nToday - Tuesday: Light Rain North of I-90\n\nLight rain showers have moved into northern portions of the Upper \nMississippi Valley early this afternoon along the leading edge of a \nmid level vorticity lobe moving through the North Central Plains. \nShowers across our area have remained light overall given the low \nlevel dry air and sub-1 inch pwats across much of the region. \nDeeper, more quality moisture remains well to our west across the \nSiouxland and into the Central Plains where stronger moisture \ntransport has also set up. As the vorticity lobe and associated\nweak surface low move off to the northeast, the showers will \ngradually lift into Northwest/North Central Wisconsin through \nthe overnight hours into early tomorrow morning. Though a few \nsprinkles may linger across our area, a mostly dry forecast is \nexpected for tomorrow. With dense cloud cover expected through \ntomorrow evening, lows for tonight have been tempered a bit as \nradiational cooling shouldn't be as effective. Also dropped \ntemperatures a few degrees for tomorrow with continued cloud \ncover likely limiting heating, similar to today. \n\nWednesday - Thursday: Warmer, Increased Rain Chances \n\nThough upper level flow looks to become more zonal by mid-week, a \nsubtle shortwave trough/trough axis is expected to move to our \nnorth late Wednesday into Thursday with weakly west/southwest \nflow ahead of this. At the surface, a weak but deepening surface\nlow will push east across the Canadian Plains and into Ontario.\nA cold front will extend to the south into Northern Plains \nearly Wednesday morning. As this trough axis and corresponding \ncold front move east, they will run into a more moist airmass \nwith deeper northward flowing gulf moisture. This influx of gulf\nmoisture into the region from southerly low level flow could \nallow for precipitable water values to climb upwards of 1.5 \ninches - roughly 150-200% of normal or 2 standard deviations \nhigher than average for late October around here. With semi- \nfavorable upper level synoptics, low level forcing from the \nsurface front, and decent moisture, this looks to be our best \nchance for rainfall overnight Wednesday into Thursday. Even with\nthe decent moisture for October standards, the very progressive\nnature of the front will keep any heavy rain concerns at bay \nwith most locations likely only seeing up to 0.25 inches with \nthis. Once the front moves through, rain chances should largely \ncome to an end with dry conditions for Thursday. \n\nTemperatures Wednesday will be the warmest of the long-term with \nhighs in the low to mid 70s, which could break records at both \nRochester and La Crosse. Post frontal temperatures Thursday will be \nback into the 60s. \n\nFriday - The Weekend: Off/On Rain, Mild Temps Continue \n\nConfidence in weekend precipitation chances wane a bit more as they \nwill rely more on an appendage moving through the southwest \nflow aloft - away from a broad upper low across the Western \nCONUS. A broad wave of moisture is also expected to push north \ninto the Upper Mississippi Valley by the weekend. In general, \nsubtle differences in how this system evolves over the coming \ndays will play a roll in how much precipitation we see across \nthe area this weekend. Precipitation chances look to peak late \nFriday into Saturday but continue off and on through the weekend\ninto Monday. Expect these to be tailored a bit more as details \ncome more into focus. Temperatures through this period will \nremain mild/slightly above normal in the 60s.",
